  /**
   * List account transactions
   *
   * <p>Date-range read of an account's transactions, sorted by <code>effective_date</code> descending, then <code>id</code> descending, in pages of up to 500. <code>date_from</code> and <code>date_to</code> are calendar dates, inclusive, in the bank's own time zone with nothing converted. They apply to <code>effective_date</code>: for a booked transaction its booking date, else its value date, else the date it was first seen; for a pending one its transaction date, else the date it was first seen. By default <code>date_from</code> is the start of the stored history and <code>date_to</code> has no upper bound, and booked and pending are both returned.
   *
   * <p>The first page pins which transactions are included and each is served in its current state: a transaction removed while you page drops out of later pages and is reported by the change feed (<code>sync_transactions</code>). <code>page_token</code> continues one read and lives one hour; an expired token answers <code>410 page_expired</code>, so restart the read from its original query. To keep a copy up to date, use the change feed instead of re-reading ranges.
   *
   * <p>Response headers: <code>X-Bank-Last-Synced-At</code> and <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code>.
   *
   * <p>Needs an API key with the Bank data permission (<code>bank-data</code>): read for this operation. A key with full access does not include it: grant it explicitly when you create the key, or the call answers <code>403 bank_data_access_denied</code>. A sandbox application reads its Test Bank data with the key alone. A request without an API key (client credentials) is refused on raw data with the same <code>403</code>.
   *
   * <p>{@code GET /v3/bank/accounts/{account_id}/transactions/}
   *
   * @param accountId The account id.
   * @param params the request parameters
   * @param options per-request options that override the client options
   * @return the response body
   */
  public BankTransactionPage listTransactions(String accountId, BankListTransactionsParams params, RequestOptions options) {
    return client.execute(listTransactionsRequest(accountId, params), options, LIST_TRANSACTIONS_RESPONSE).data();
  }

